.TH SDR 1
.SH NAME
sdr/fm, sdr/am \- demodulate a software-defined radio's samples
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sdr/fm
[
.B -nw
] [
.B -c
.I chans
] [
.B -d
.I µs
] [
.B -g
.I gain
] [
.B -o
.I rate
] [
.B -r
.I rate
]
.PP
.B sdr/am
[
.B -b
.I hertz
] [
.B -c
.I chans
] [
.B -g
.I gain
] [
.B -o
.I rate
] [
.B -r
.I rate
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Each reads a radio's samples on its standard input, as
.IR sdrfs (4)'s
.B iq
gives them (8-bit unsigned I then Q), and writes the sound they carry on
its standard output, as
.IR audio (3)
takes it: 16-bit little-endian samples,
.I chans
of each, the same sound in every channel.
The radio is tuned to the station; what is a little off (a frequency
error, an offset in ppm) is heard all the same.
They are filters: they read until end of file and keep no state
elsewhere.
.PP
.I Fm
demodulates FM: wide, broadcast FM, by default (or
.BR -w ),
and narrow with
.B -n
(marine VHF, 2 m amateur, NOAA weather, the land mobile bands).
The samples are summed to about 240k a second; narrow, the channel
(8 kHz either side) is filtered out of them and they are decimated to
about 48k.
The angle each turns from the last is the sound.
Wide, it is de-emphasised,
.B -d
microseconds: 50, as broadcast in most of the world, 75 in the Americas,
0 none (narrow has none unless asked).
Wide, the sound is kept below 15 kHz (above are the stereo pilot and
subcarriers, and RDS), and below 0.4 of the rate out; it is mono.
Full deviation (75 kHz wide, 5 kHz narrow) is 0.7 of full scale.
.PP
.I Am
demodulates AM: the air band (118 to 137 MHz), and the short and medium
wave broadcasts through direct sampling or an upconverter.
The samples are summed to about 240k a second, the channel
.RB ( -b
hertz either side, 5000) is filtered out and they are decimated to
about 48k.
Each one's magnitude over the carrier's (its magnitude averaged over a
tenth of a second) is the sound, so the level out does not follow the
signal's strength; full modulation is 0.7 of full scale.
.PP
Both take:
.TP
.BI -r " rate"
the rate in: what
.I sdrfs
is set to (2048000, its default), with k or M taken (2.4M).
.TP
.BI -o " rate"
the rate out: 8000, what
.B /dev/audio
plays until told otherwise; set both higher for broadcast FM (it
takes 8000, 11025, 22050 and 44100).
.TP
.BI -c " chans"
the channels out (2, as
.IR audio (3)).
.TP
.BI -g " gain"
the volume multiplied; past full scale is clipped.
.PP
A rate out the rate in cannot give is refused.
.SH EXAMPLES
A broadcast station, to the speaker:
.IP
.EX
; sdrfs source localhost
; echo 'freq 98.5M
rate 2.4M
gain 20' > /mnt/sdr/ctl
; echo rate 44100 > /dev/audioctl
; sdr/fm -r 2.4M -o 44100 < /mnt/sdr/iq > /dev/audio
.EE
.PP
The air band's emergency frequency:
.IP
.EX
; echo freq 121.5M > /mnt/sdr/ctl
; sdr/am -r 2.4M < /mnt/sdr/iq > /dev/audio
.EE
.PP
A recording made once, demodulated later:
.IP
.EX
; sdr/fm -n -c 1 < /tmp/marine.cu8 > /tmp/marine.pcm
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/sdr/fm.b
.br
.B /appl/cmd/sdr/am.b
.br
.B /appl/lib/sdrdsp.b
.SH SEE ALSO
